    "result_text": "The test passes and the build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe task was to validate the E.164 telephone's national-number length against its country code (`+61` \u2192 9 national digits, `+1` \u2192 10), rejecting mismatches with a 400.\n\nI placed this logic where telephone canonicalization already lives \u2014 `TelephoneNormalizer.toE164(...)` \u2014 rather than spreading it across the pipeline steps. This keeps the single source of truth for \"what is a valid E.164 number here,\" so both `NormalizeOwnerTelephone` (which turns a `null` result into an `InvalidTelephoneException` \u2192 400) and `EnsureUniqueTelephone` (which normalizes stored numbers before comparing) automatically agree on the stricter rule.\n\nThe change:\n- Added a small `NATIONAL_LENGTHS` map (`\"61\"\u21929`, `\"1\"\u219210`).\n- `toE164` now returns `null` (i.e. invalid) when the national part doesn't match the required length for the recognised country code, on top of the existing 8\u201315 digit shape check.\n- Two focused private helpers: `hasValidNationalLength` (the rule) and `countryCode` (longest-recognised-prefix match), each with a single responsibility. Unrecognised country codes fall back to the general E.164 shape check, preserving existing behaviour.\n\nFor the failing case `+61 123`: it strips to `+61123`, national part is `123` (3 digits, not 9) \u2192 rejected as 400. The existing valid `04XXXXXXXX` mobiles normalize to `+61` + 9 national digits and remain accepted.\n\n`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS.",
